From 655aa28bd3404e7ae045fe077bb095e18d7603f8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 01:55:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] system: copy the diagnostics to a USB drive
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

What the diagnostics save goes to the folder hades-diag of the boot
partition. On a USB drive Windows shows that partition (LIBREELEC); on a
system installed on a disk inside the PC it does not, and the only way out
was Kodi's file manager, without any check of what it copied.

System > Submit Log > "Copy the diagnostics to a USB drive" asks
/usr/lib/hades-diag/to-usb.sh for the drives it can copy to (USB drives,
removable ones and SD cards that are mounted with writing allowed, never a
disk inside the PC), offers them by their names and what they have free,
with nothing preselected, and has the copy made: into hades-diag on the
drive, in a folder named after the date and time, read back from the drive
and compared file by file before it says where it is. Each refusal of the
script is said for what it is (nothing to copy and how to turn the
diagnostics on, no room, a drive that gives back something else, one that
cannot be written, busy, no memory). The script is run outside the
environment of Kodi, as the one of the internal disks is. The button is not
shown on a system without the script.

The help also says the way that needs no screen: a USB drive with a folder
named hades-diag at its top, plugged in while the system runs, gets the
same copy by itself (hades-diag-usb@.service).

Texts in English and the three Spanish ones (tú in Spain and Mexico, vos
in Argentina; "sitio", "espacio" and "lugar" for room), numbered from 33100
so that they meet none of the other changes of this version.
